687search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: last week; Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease therapeutic programs recently halted or discontinued ... ^{82}* GLPG2737, a small-molecule cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator inhibitor designed to reduce chloride-driven fluid secretion,^{83,84} entered the phase 2 MANGROVE study in ADPKD but was terminated after failing to show efficacy (NCT04578548, Galapagos, clinicaltrials.gov, 2024).\n^{74} First-in-human studies by Mironid are anticipated to assess safety, pharmacokinetics, and pharmacodynamics. If efficacy and safety are confirmed, PDE4 activation could provide a disease-modifying therapy to slow progression of ADPKD.\n\n## Therapies Recently Stalled or Terminated\n\nThe ADPKD pipeline is broad, yet several programs have been halted due to safety or lack of efficacy (Table 3). These experiences underscore the importance of predictive preclinical models and validated biomarkers for therapies requiring long-term use.\n\n### Table 3.\n\nAutosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease therapeutic programs recently halted or discontinued\n\nProgram  | Class/Target  | Rationale  | Trial Outcome/Reason for Stop  | Take-home\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nGLPG2737  | CFTR inhibitor  | Reduces cyst fluid secretion (antisecretory)  | Phase 2 terminated for lack of efficacy; no positive signal reported  | CFTR pathway needs better agents or trial design\nVenglustat  | GCS inhibitor  | Modulates glycosphingolipid metabolism  | STAGED-PKD stopped for futility; no total kidney volume benefit  | Strong rodent data did not translate to human TKV.\n^{76} Tesevatinib (KD019), a multikinase inhibitor with EGF receptor and Src activity, showed limited TKV benefit^{77} and off-target toxicity (NCT03203642, Sanofi, clinicaltrials.gov, 2023).^{78}* Lixivaptan, a selective V2 receptor antagonist,^{79} entered phase 3 testing as an alternative to tolvaptan. Development was paused after a case of significant transaminase elevation raised concern for class-related hepatotoxicity, eroding confidence in a clear safety advantage (press release: Centessa 2022).^{80}* Bardoxolone methyl, an Nrf2 activator with anti-inflammatory and metabolic effects,^{81} produced transient eGFR increases in ADPKD patients, likely hemodynamic in nature, without clear structural benefit. Concerns over fluid retention and cardiovascular safety prompted discontinuation (non–peer-reviewed abstract: Anand et al., Kidney Week 2024).^{82}* GLPG2737, a small-molecule cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator inhibitor designed to reduce chloride-driven fluid secretion,^{83,84} entered the phase 2 MANGROVE study in ADPKD but was terminated after failing to show efficacy (NCT04578548, Galapagos, clinicaltrials.gov, 2024).^{85}* Somatostatin analogues have been evaluated as potential disease-modifying therapies in ADPKD. Octreotide was tested in the a long-acting somatostatin on disease progression in nephropathy,^{86} and a long-acting somatostatin on disease progression in nephropathy trials,^{87} followed by lanreotide in the larger developing interventions to halt progression 1 and lanreotide in PKD studies.^{88} Although these agents reduced TKV, they did not demonstrate a meaningful effect on eGFR decline.^{88} However, somatostatin analogues have therapeutic value in patients with severe polycystic liver disease.^{89}\n\nThese halted efforts highlight that future success requires standardized orthologous models for preclinical testing, surrogate end points linked to long-term outcomes, and early safety screening to focus investment on the most promising agents.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombining Gene Therapy with Current Modulator Treatments for Cystic Fibrosis: A Promising Area of Research (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13516476/)\nciteturn687search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Moreover, type II correctors, such as C4 and core-corr-II, are examples of molecules that interact with NBD2 to improve protein folding (Table 1) [112]. ... However, a recent type II corrector, named GLPG2737, has been identified (Table 1) [128,129]. ... Currently, the development of this compound has been discontinued regarding CF [129].\nThis corrector improves p.Phe508del maturation and trafficking alone and while used in combination with a type I corrector such as lumacaftor [128]. However, comparison of chloride efflux in CF cells from donors treated with GLPG2737 alone or in triple combination showed a slight decrease in channel function when this modulator was not combined with a potentiator [129]. Currently, the development of this compound has been discontinued regarding CF [129].\n\nGalicaftor (SION-2222) is another corrector under investigation in a phase I CT (Table 1) (NCT07035990). This type I corrector has a similar structure to lumacaftor and tezacaftor and also targets the MSD1 of the CFTR protein [104,130,131]. This compound was evaluated in multiple CTs as a triple combination with either Navocaftor/ABBV-119 or Navocaftor/ABBV-567 (NCT04853368, NCT05538585, NCT03969888, NCT03045523, NCT03119649). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIon channel ligands in clinical development – Quarterly review (Q2 2024) – ION CHANNEL LIBRARY (https://www.ionchannellibrary.com/expert_opinions/ion-channel-ligands-in-clinical-development-quarterly-review-q2-2024/)\nciteturn687search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; The first asset is Galapagos’s GLPG2737, which actually predates this blog as it entered my database in Q1 of 2021, although I did mention a collaboration between Abbvie and Galapagos including this CFTR corrector in the Q2 2022 blog. As quite often happens in drug discovery, the publication of the discovery, optimisation and structure of this clinical asset likely occurred once it was discontinued, which apparently happened after Ph II trials as revealed by a post in March from DrugHunter. ... SPL84 got IND approval earlier in Q2 2024 and the company also received an additional $8.5 million from the CF Foundation at this time, which is being used to support a current Ph II trial that is hoped to provide the first clinical PoC for an ASO approach to treat CF.\nb. Respiratory disease\n\nMixed news on the cystic fibrosis front this quarter, with one historical program finally being abandoned while more recent ligands make progress.\n\nThe first asset is Galapagos’s GLPG2737, which actually predates this blog as it entered my database in Q1 of 2021, although I did mention a collaboration between Abbvie and Galapagos including this CFTR corrector in the Q2 2022 blog. As quite often happens in drug discovery, the publication of the discovery, optimisation and structure of this clinical asset likely occurred once it was discontinued, which apparently happened after Ph II trials as revealed by a post in March from DrugHunter. The ligand is a structurally unique type C2 co-corrector that worked well with a potentiator and C1 corrector preclinically to improve lung function and reduce sweat production, but apparently only produced marginal gains clinically which could not match those produced by the various Vertex drug combinations currently on the market and undergoing clinical trials. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPowerPoint Presentation (https://assets.roche.com/f/176343/x/f513f69de2/pharma_day_20240930_final_online_v02.pdf)\nciteturn687search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Since Pharma Day 2023, 17 NMEs were added and 26 NMEs removed, resulting in a higher impact portfolio ... Waterfall-style pipeline change chart from Q2 2023 to Sep 30 2024. ... Intermediate green bars indicate additions and red bars indicate removals across periods labeled Q3 23 changes, Q4 23 changes, Q1 2